@RNF-01
Feature: Performance budget for a typical track and a 15-track album (H2)
  As a user who checks whole albums
  I want a typical track analysed in under 3 seconds and a 15-track album in under 20 seconds
  So that checking a download takes less time than listening to its first song

  Spec: specs/contracts/performance.md (fixtures, what is timed, reference
  machine, harness, report, levers, acceptance T0 to T14, manual checks M1 to
  M3, G1 questions Q1 to Q14, decided defaults D1 to D10, baseline).
  Decisions: ADR-0006 and ADR-0034 (parallel tracks), ADR-0010 (window
  sampling), ADR-0007, ADR-0008 and ADR-0044 (result cache), ADR-0019
  (justfile), ADR-0022 (corpus outside the repository), and ADR-0046
  (docs/adr/0046-performance-budget-opt-in-std-only-harness-in-release-not-in-ci.md,
  proposed; performance.md Q14).

  How the scenarios run (performance.md sections 5 and 9):
    "The harness" is src-tauri/tests/perf.rs. Scenarios marked "(timed)" are
    its ignored tests: they run only through "just perf", in a release build,
    on the owner's machine, never in CI; their figures go in the feature PR.
    Every other scenario is checked by its smoke test, which CI runs in
    "just check-app" and which asserts nothing about any time.
  Fixtures (performance.md section 2) are generated by the harness in a new
  folder under the system temporary folder, never in the repository or the
  audio corpus, and deleted when the test ends:
    "typical" is a FLAC, 44100 Hz, 16-bit, stereo, 15 872 000 frames
      (359.909 s) of full-band white noise, between 38 000 000 and
      42 000 000 bytes; its truth is "real";
    "the album" is the folder "Álbum ″Ñandú″" with 15 byte copies of
      "typical", named "01 – Canción ‘x’.flac" to "15 – Canción ‘x’.flac";
    "the corrupt album" is the album with "08 – Canción ‘x’.flac" replaced by
      the first half of the bytes of "typical";
    "the tiny set" is two FLAC 16/44.1 stereo files of 438 272 frames in a
      folder "Álbum ″Ñandú″", for the smoke test (performance.md 2.3 allows
      a shorter whole-block length, above 5 s, if the smoke test exceeds its
      60 s budget).
  The "machine:" line is printed only by the "just perf" recipe, before the
  harness runs; it is not part of the harness output, so the smoke test and a
  direct "cargo test ... --ignored" run never show it.
  "A figure" (except the single-run report rows T7 and T11) is the median of
  5 measured runs after 1 warm-up run, in whole
  milliseconds; the first and the maximum run are reported with it.
  Cache modes (performance.md 3.2): "off" uses no result cache; "cold" uses a
  new empty cache file in the test's temporary folder for every run; "warm"
  uses the cache file left by one cold run of the same folder.
  "The gated workers" is min(worker_count(), 7): the reference machine of
  performance.md section 4 has 8 logical CPUs, so worker_count() gives 7.

  # --- The budget (timed) ---

  Scenario Outline: The typical track is analysed in under 3 seconds (timed)
    Given the harness has generated "typical" alone in a folder
    When "just perf" times run_analysis on that folder with the cache <mode>
    Then the figure of row "T1" with cache "<mode>" is below 3000 ms
    And its result is "pass"

    Examples:
      | mode |
      | off  |
      | cold |

  Scenario Outline: A 15-track album is analysed in under 20 seconds (timed)
    Given the harness has generated the album
    When "just perf" times analyze_folder_cached on the album with the gated workers and the cache <mode>
    Then the figure of row "T2" with cache "<mode>" is below 20000 ms
    And its result is "pass"

    Examples:
      | mode |
      | off  |
      | cold |

  Scenario: Timed runs do real work (timed)
    Given the harness has generated "typical" and the album
    When "just perf" times rows "T1" and "T2" in both cache modes
    Then every run, the warm-up included, ends with "finished" and cancelled false
    And done equals total equals the number of files of its folder
    And every trackDone has verdict "real", no error, sample rate 44100 and a duration within 1 ms of 359909 ms
    And errors is 0
    And a run that breaks any of these fails the suite whatever its time

  Scenario Outline: Which figures fail the run
    The report is built by one function of the harness from the figures
    (performance.md 6.3), so the smoke test checks it with given figures.
    Given the report function is given every figure below its target except <figures>
    Then every row of the report is printed
    And the last report line is "<last line>"

    Examples:
      | figures                                          | last line                       |
      | a T1 cold median of 3000 ms                      | result: FAIL (T1 cold)          |
      | a T1 cold median of 2999 ms                      | result: pass                    |
      | a T1 cold median of 3000 ms and a T2 off median of 20000 ms | result: FAIL (T1 cold, T2 off) |
      | a T9 median of 501 ms                            | result: pass                    |

  Scenario: A missed gate fails "just perf" after the whole report is printed (timed)
    Given a run of "just perf" whose report ends with a "result: FAIL" line
    Then the timed suite fails after printing every row
    And "just perf" exits with status 1

  Scenario: The fixtures are what the budget means
    Given the harness has generated its fixtures
    Then "typical" is a FLAC of 44100 Hz, 16 bits, 2 channels and 15 872 000 frames
    And its size is at least 38 000 000 and at most 42 000 000 bytes
    And the report's "fixtures:" line gives its size in bytes and its frames
    And no run is timed before every fixture has passed these checks

  Scenario Outline: A fixture outside its size window stops the harness before timing
    Checked through the pure function check_fixture(id, bytes, min, max) of
    perf.rs, which the smoke test calls with given values (performance.md
    section 2 and T5).
    When check_fixture is called with "typical", <bytes>, 38000000 and 42000000
    Then it returns <result>
    And the timed suite, which calls it with each fixture's real size, prints an Err line, times no run and fails

    Examples:
      | bytes    | result                                                                          |
      | 43000000 | Err "perf: fixture typical is 43000000 bytes, outside [38000000, 42000000]" |
      | 38000000 | Ok                                                                              |
      | 42000000 | Ok                                                                              |

  Scenario: The fixtures declare their length, so a truncated copy is a corrupt stream
    Given the harness has generated its fixtures with flacenc
    Then the STREAMINFO total-samples field of "typical" is 15872000, not 0
    And in the smoke test the field of each tiny file is 438272
    And otherwise the harness prints "perf: fixture typical has STREAMINFO total samples <value>, expected 15872000" and times no run

  # --- Reliability (RNF-04) ---

  @RNF-04
  Scenario: A corrupt track in the album keeps the album within the budget (timed)
    Given the harness has generated the corrupt album
    When "just perf" times analyze_folder_cached on it with the gated workers and the cache off
    Then the figure of row "T4" is below 20000 ms
    And every run has errors 1
    And "08 – Canción ‘x’.flac" has verdict "error" with the error "corruptStream"
    And the 14 other tracks have verdict "real"

  @RNF-04
  Scenario: The folder report says which format an error came from
    Given a temporary folder holding the first tiny file and the second one truncated to half its bytes
    When the smoke test runs the folder mode on it
    Then the folder report shows "tracks 2" and "errors 1"
    And its "flac" format row has tracks 2 and errors 1

  # --- Unicode names (RNF-09) ---

  @RNF-09
  Scenario: Unicode names are timed like any other name (timed)
    Given the album's folder is "Álbum ″Ñandú″" and its files are "01 – Canción ‘x’.flac" to "15 – Canción ‘x’.flac"
    When "just perf" times rows "T1" and "T2"
    Then every trackDone path ends with its file name exactly
    And the gates of rows "T1" and "T2" apply to these runs unchanged

  @RNF-09
  Scenario: A folder whose path has spaces, an ampersand and Unicode characters is timed
    Given a temporary folder "Álbum ″Ñandú″ & co" holding the tiny set
    When the smoke test runs the folder mode on it
    Then the folder report shows "tracks 2", "errors 0" and "flac 2, wav 0, mp3 0, m4a 0, opus 0"
    And it has one format row, for "flac", with tracks 2 and errors 0
    And the output contains neither the folder name, nor either file name, nor any component of 4 or more characters of the folder's absolute path

  # --- Result cache (RF-11) ---

  Scenario: The first run with an empty cache stays within the budget (timed)
    Given a new empty cache file in the test's temporary folder before every run
    When "just perf" times rows "T1" and "T2" with the cache "cold"
    Then every run ends with cache "ok" and fromCache 0
    And the gates of rows "T1" and "T2" hold in this mode as in mode "off"

  Scenario: A warm run is reported with an indicative target (timed)
    Given one cold run of the album has filled the cache file
    When "just perf" times the album again with the cache "warm"
    Then every run ends with cache "ok" and fromCache 15
    And row "T6" has kind "indicative" and target ms the smaller of 1000 and a tenth of the T2 off median, rounded to the nearest ms
    And its result is "met" or "missed" and never fails the suite

  # --- Reported figures (indicative) ---

  Scenario Outline: Indicative figures never fail the run
    Given a report of the harness (the smoke test's, or a run of "just perf")
    Then row "<id>" with case "<case>" has kind "<kind>" and target ms "<target>"
    And its result is "met" or "missed" for kind "indicative" and "-" for kind "report", and never changes the "result:" line

    Examples:
      | id  | case                       | kind       | target |
      | T6  | album                      | indicative | min(1000, T2 off median / 10) |
      | T7  | album                      | indicative | 20000  |
      | T8  | hires track                | indicative | 3000   |
      | T8  | wav track                  | indicative | 3000   |
      | T9  | cancel latency, album      | indicative | 500    |
      | T10 | spectrogram, typical track | indicative | 2000   |
      | T11 | album, sequential          | report     | -      |

  Scenario: Cancellation latency is reported (timed)
    Cancelled true means done < total, so it needs fewer workers than tracks:
    the gated workers (at most 7) and the album (15 tracks) guarantee it.
    Given the harness runs the album with the gated workers and the cache off
    And the run is cancelled when its first trackDone arrives
    When "just perf" measures the time from the cancel to the return of the run
    Then row "T9" reports its median against the indicative target 500 ms
    And every run ends cancelled true with a trackDone for every trackStarted

  Scenario: The smoke test's cancelled run leaves a track unstarted
    Given the smoke test runs row "T9" on the tiny set with 1 worker and the cache off
    And the run is cancelled when its first trackDone arrives
    Then the run ends cancelled true with done 1 and total 2
    And every trackStarted has a trackDone
    And row "T9" shows 1 in its workers cell

  Scenario: Spectrogram time is reported (timed)
    When "just perf" times run::get_spectrogram on "typical"
    Then row "T10" reports its median against the indicative target 2000 ms
    And every spectrogram has a width and a height above 0

  # --- Harness ---

  Scenario Outline: A debug build gives no figures
    Checked through the pure function check_release_build(debug_assertions)
    of perf.rs, which the smoke test calls with given values; the timed suite
    calls it first with cfg!(debug_assertions) (performance.md D2 and T0).
    When check_release_build is called with <debug assertions>
    Then it returns <result>

    Examples:
      | debug assertions | result                                                          |
      | true             | Err "perf: figures need a release build (run: just perf)" |
      | false            | Ok                                                              |

  Scenario: The timed suite in a debug build prints the error and times nothing
    Given the timed suite is built with debug assertions
    When it runs
    Then it prints "perf: figures need a release build (run: just perf)", the Err of check_release_build(true)
    And no run is timed
    And it fails

  Scenario: The report has a fixed shape
    When the smoke test runs the synthetic suite on the tiny set with 1 warm-up and 1 measured run per row
    Then the output holds the lines "RNF-01 performance report (specs/contracts/performance.md)", "build: debug", a "workers:" line and a "fixtures:" line
    And the output has no "machine:" line
    And the table has exactly 12 rows with ids T1, T1, T2, T2, T4, T6, T7, T8, T8, T9, T10, T11 in this order
    And every "first ms", "median ms" and "max ms" cell is an integer, and every "target ms" cell is an integer or "-"
    And the last report line starts with "result: "

  Scenario: The empty state: a folder without audio gives no figures
    Given a temporary folder holding only "notes.txt"
    When the smoke test runs the folder mode on it
    Then the harness output is exactly the 3 lines "RNF-01 folder report (aggregates only, ADR-0022)", "build: debug" and "perf: NO_AUDIO_FOUND", with no "machine:" line
    And no table is printed
    And the folder mode fails

  @RNF-05
  Scenario: The folder mode never modifies the timed files
    Given a temporary folder holding the tiny set
    When the smoke test runs the folder mode on it
    Then the size, modification time and content hash of both files are unchanged
    And the folder holds exactly the files it held before

  Scenario: just perf accepts at most one argument
    Checked by hand (performance.md M3); T14 checks that the recipe holds the text.
    When I run "just perf" with the arguments "a" and "b"
    Then it prints "usage: just perf [FOLDER]"
    And it exits with status 2

  Scenario: The gate is opt-in and never runs in CI
    When the smoke test reads the justfile and the workflows as text
    Then the justfile has a "perf" recipe whose body holds "--release", "--ignored", "--test-threads=1", "--test perf", "FAKEFLAC_PERF_FOLDER" and "usage: just perf [FOLDER]"
    And none of the recipes "ci", "check", "check-fast", "test" and "check-app" names "perf" as a whole word (\bperf\b) in its dependency list or body, so "performance" does not count
    And neither ".github/workflows/ci.yml" nor ".github/workflows/release.yml" contains "just perf"
